    "body": "As an aside, but it doesn't impact your larger point: \"parser-inserted\" is defined for all script elements. (It is defined in terms of parser document, which is always either null or a document, and is set during parsing.) Basically script's \"parser-inserted\" is a more-rigorous version of \"the element was created by its node document's parser\".\r\n\r\nAnyway, for a solution to your question: yes, I think there are a few ways to do that. One way would be something like\r\n\r\n> A script element is implicitly render-blocking if the script's type is set and is equal to \"classic\", and the element is \"parser-inserted\".\r\n\r\nIn the future we should probably do a more general cleanup of all these script \"members\", as they are very old parts of the spec and thus have weird style differences (such as not having proper initial values for un-prepared scripts, or using set/unsert flags instead of true/false booleans, or using \"scare quotes\" around various properties, or using names like \"the script's type\" instead of just \"type\"). But for now something like the above seems pretty clear to me.",
    "created_at": "2022-04-26T15:28:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-04-26T15:30:20Z",
